<section>
  <div class="sec-label">Third step</div>
  <h2>From building to repeating</h2>
  <p class="lede">When an assignment works, do not write it again from scratch. Save it with its sources, boundaries and tests. It no longer depends on how inspired you feel that day.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div><b>Save the version that worked</b><span>Keep the assignment and an example of the approved outcome.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Name the job, not the tool</b><span>“Reply to reviews” is better than “my favourite prompt”.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Test a normal case and an exception</b><span>The exception shows whether it knows when to stop and ask.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Update it with a date</b><span>Procedures age too; record what changed and why.</span></div></li>
  </ol>
  <div class="prompt"><div class="prompt-head"><span class="prompt-name">Example 04 · Saved assignment</span><button class="copy">Copy</button></div><pre>Turn the assignment we have just approved into a reusable instruction.

Keep its sources, objective, deliverables, boundaries and final tests. Add an exceptions section describing the cases where you must stop and ask me.

Give it a name that describes the job rather than the tool. Include one normal test case and one that should trigger a stop.

Do not change any criterion without flagging it. Return a dated version and a summary of the decisions you preserved.</pre></div>
  <div class="note"><b>No demonstration needed</b><p>Show a short list of saved assignments. People understand the idea when they see names of real jobs, not a technical explanation.</p></div>
</section>
</div>

